---

<ArticleLayout
  title="What You Need to Rent a Car in Portugal | TripAxle"
  description="The documents, licence, card and age requirements for renting a car in Portugal — what the law sets, what each supplier sets, and what to bring to the desk."
  eyebrow="Booking Basics"
  summary="Two different rulebooks decide whether you get the keys: Portuguese law, and the rental company's own terms. This separates them so you know which one to check."
  updated="2026-09-03"
  published="2026-09-03"
  verified="2026-09-03"
  image={images.coimbra}
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  toc={toc}
  related={related}
  sources={cite("imtForeignLicence", "imtIdp", "yourEurope")}
  sourcesNote="Licence questions here are answered from the Portuguese licensing authority and the European Commission's own guidance. Age limits, card rules and years-held requirements are commercial terms, not law, so no source can settle them for you — only your supplier's current terms can."
  trustNote="This guide deliberately states no universal minimum age and no universal IDP rule, because neither exists. Where it says 'varies', that is the finding, not a hedge."
>
  <QuickAnswer slot="answer">
    <p>
      To collect a rental car in Portugal you need a valid driving licence you
      are entitled to use here, photo identification, and an accepted payment
      card <strong>in the main driver's own name</strong>. Everything else —
      minimum age, how long you must have held the licence, whether a debit card
      is accepted, whether an International Driving Permit is demanded — is set
      by the rental company, not by Portuguese law, and differs between
      suppliers. Check your own booking terms.
    </p>
  </QuickAnswer>

  <AtAGlance items={glance} />

  <h2 id="licence">Your driving licence</h2>
  <p>
    Licences issued in an EU or EEA country are recognised across the EU, so a
    licence from another member state lets you drive in Portugal on the same
    terms as at home. The European Commission's own guidance confirms the
    mutual-recognition position.
  </p>
  <p>
    If your licence was issued outside the EU or EEA, the Portuguese licensing
    authority (IMT) allows you to drive on a foreign driving title for
    <strong>185 days</strong> following entry into Portugal, before you
    establish residence here — that is, for the whole of any normal holiday. The
    licence must be valid and you must meet the Portuguese minimum age for the
    vehicle category.
  </p>
  <p>
    That is the legal position. It is separate from whether a particular rental
    company will accept your licence, which is a contractual matter and can be
    stricter.
  </p>

  <h2 id="idp">When an International Driving Permit is worth carrying</h2>
  <p>
    An International Driving Permit is a translation booklet, not a licence.
    IMT is explicit that it is only valid alongside the national licence that
    supports it, that it is issued for a maximum of one year, and that it is
    aimed at driving in countries <em>outside</em> the EU and EEA that are
    signatories to the international road traffic conventions. The European
    Commission notes separately that IDPs are not regulated at EU level and may
    not be recognised in every member state.
  </p>
  <Callout title="So do you need one for Portugal?">
    We will not give you a universal yes or no, because the honest answer has
    two halves. As a matter of Portuguese licensing law, a valid foreign licence
    is what permits you to drive. As a matter of contract, some rental companies
    require an IDP alongside a non-EU licence — particularly one not written in
    the Latin alphabet — and will refuse the car without it. An IDP is cheap
    and quick to obtain at home; if your licence was issued outside the EU/EEA,
    carrying one removes an argument you cannot win at a desk in a foreign
    country at midnight.
  </Callout>
  <p>
    If you do get one, carry it <em>with</em> the original licence. On its own
    it is worth nothing.
  </p>

  <h2 id="identity">Passport or ID card</h2>
  <p>
    Expect to show photo identification separate from the licence. For EU and
    EEA visitors a national identity card normally serves; for everyone else
    that means a passport. Desks routinely take a copy or scan. Bring the actual
    document — a photo of it on your phone is not reliably accepted.
  </p>

  <h2 id="card">The card, and whose name is on it</h2>
  <p>
    This is where most refused pickups happen. The security deposit is placed as
    a pre-authorisation hold on a card that must normally be
    <strong>in the name of the lead driver</strong> and physically present, with
    the name embossed or printed on it. A card belonging to a spouse, parent or
    employer is generally not accepted, however obviously legitimate.
  </p>
  <p>
    Whether a debit card is accepted at all varies by company, by branch and
    sometimes by vehicle class, and acceptance often comes with extra
    conditions.
    <a href="/portugal-car-rental-with-debit/">Renting with a debit card</a>
    covers how to check before you pay for anything, and
    <a href="/portugal-car-rental-deposits-and-card-requirements/">deposits and card requirements</a>
    explains how the hold itself works.
  </p>

  <h2 id="age">Age and licence-held requirements</h2>
  <p>
    There is no single Portuguese rental age. Suppliers set their own minimum
    age, their own minimum period since the licence was issued, and their own
    young-driver surcharge, and some also apply a maximum age or restrict which
    vehicle groups younger drivers may take. Larger and premium groups usually
    carry higher thresholds than the smallest cars.
  </p>
  <p>
    Because these are commercial terms rather than legal rules, the only
    reliable way to answer them is to open the terms attached to the quote you
    are looking at and search for "age". Do it before you pay, not at the desk:
    a young-driver fee that appears at pickup is usually payable and rarely
    refundable.
  </p>

  <h2 id="additional-drivers">Additional drivers</h2>
  <p>
    Anyone else who will drive must normally be registered on the rental
    agreement and present their own licence and identification at the desk.
    Driving the car while unregistered typically voids the damage cover in the
    contract, which is a far more expensive problem than the additional-driver
    fee. Some rates include one additional driver; most charge per day.
  </p>
  <p>
    The same age and licence-held thresholds usually apply to additional drivers
    as to the lead driver.
  </p>

  <h2 id="at-the-desk">What to physically bring to the desk</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The lead driver's licence — the plastic card, plus the paper counterpart if your country still issues one.</li>
    <li>Passport or national ID card for the lead driver.</li>
    <li>The payment card in the lead driver's name, physically present.</li>
    <li>Your booking confirmation or voucher, including the reference number.</li>
    <li>Every additional driver's licence and ID.</li>
    <li>Your International Driving Permit, if you obtained one.</li>
    <li>Flight number, if the booking was made with one — it is what links a delayed arrival to your reservation.</li>
    <li>Proof of any third-party excess policy you bought, if you want to be able to point at it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="checklist">Pre-departure checklist</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Check the licence expiry date. An expired licence ends the rental before it starts.</li>
    <li>Confirm the name on your booking matches the name on your licence and your card, character for character.</li>
    <li>Open the supplier's terms and find the age, licence-held and card clauses. Screenshot them.</li>
    <li>Confirm your card's available limit will absorb the deposit hold on top of the rental charge.</li>
    <li>Register additional drivers at booking rather than at the desk where possible.</li>
    <li>If your licence is non-EU, decide on an IDP while you are still in your own country.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Once the paperwork is settled, the next decisions are financial:
    <a href="/portugal-car-rental-cost/">what the rental actually costs</a> and
    <a href="/portugal-car-rental-insurance/">which cover you are buying</a>.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>
